.TH WHEREIS 1
.SH NAME
whereis \- locate the program, source and manual page of a command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whereis
[\ \-sbmu\ ] [\ \-SBM\ dir\ ...\ \-f\ ] name ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whereis
looks for the program, the source and the manual pages of each
.IR name ,
in the places they are usually kept, and prints what it finds on one
line:
.PP
.nf
	$ whereis cc strcpy
	cc: /usr/bin/cc /usr/man/cat1/cc.1
	strcpy: /usr/man/cat3/string.3
.fi
.PP
A path before the name is left off, and so is a suffix of one letter or
digit, such as .c or .1, so that whereis can be given a file name. The
places looked in are:
.TP 12
programs
/bin, /usr/bin, /etc, /lib, /usr/lib, /usr/lib/cc, /usr/local/bin,
/usr/local and /usr/games: executable files of that name
.TP
manuals
/usr/man/cat\fIN\fP/\fIname\fP.\fIN\fP and /usr/man/man\fIN\fP/\fIname\fP.\fIN\fP,
for each section N; and, from the index /usr/man/whatis, the page that
describes
.I name
when it has another name, as string(3) does strcpy
.TP
sources
/usr/src/cmd, /usr/src/lib, /usr/src/local and /usr/src: a file
.IR name ,
.IR name .c,
.IR name .s,
.IR name .y,
.IR name .l,
.IR name .f
or
.IR name .sh,
or a directory
.I name
.PP
The options are:
.TP 6
.B \-b
Look only for programs.
.TP
.B \-m
Look only for manual pages.
.TP
.B \-s
Look only for sources.
.TP
.B \-u
Print only the names that are unusual: those that do not have exactly
one of each kind asked for.
.TP
.B \-B
.PD 0
.TP
.B \-M
.TP
.B \-S
.PD
Look for programs, manuals or sources in the directories that follow,
up to a
.BR \-f ,
instead of the usual places.
.TP
.B \-f
End a list of directories.
.SH EXAMPLES
To list the commands in /usr/bin that do not have exactly one manual
page:
.PP
.nf
	cd /usr/bin
	whereis \-m \-u *
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is 0, whether or not anything was found, and 2 after a
mistake in the options.
.SH SEE ALSO
man(1), intro(1)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B whereis
command appeared in 3.0BSD.
.SH AUTHORS
This whereis was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
whereis looks only in the places it knows of, unlike the shell, which
looks where PATH says.
.PP
A name with a suffix of one character, such as a command named
.BR x.y ,
loses it.
